		{
			"id": "114916141995819918",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://mastodon.online/@polx/114916141955673215",
			"author": {
				"name": "Paul L",
				"url": "https://mastodon.online/@polx",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/111/500/432/916/497/890/original/7f330d246008069d.jpeg"
			},
			"date": "2025-07-25T22:06:09.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> a few years ago I had to spend 3 months of development because <a href=\"https://mastodon.online/tags/safari\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>safari</span></a>’s date parsing had changed between versions of <a href=\"https://mastodon.online/tags/ios\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>ios</span></a> making our server emitted dates break… </p><p>This was the perfect illustration why flawed standards such as iso8601 are a problem. And this the reason why date parsing is a nightmare… </p><p>Just be strict. Period.<br>And accept that this is politically incorrect.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
